you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's program global environmental  action a top priority as u.s  climate envoy john kerry becomes the  highest ranking biden administration  official  to visit moscow led light bulbs  smart appliances and hydrogen boilers  what the future  could look like in a net zero britain  and find out what it's like to spend  months on a ship  frozen in the arctic ice all in the name  of climate science  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes happening to  our world right now  and we meet those coming up with the  solutions  now they're two of the world's biggest  emitters of greenhouse gases so action  on climate change taken by the united  states and russia  has huge implications for the rest of  the world today  u.s climate envoy john kerry is in  moscow  meeting russia's foreign minister sergey  lavrov ahead of the cop  26 summit in november well russia is a  signatory to the international paris  climate deal agreed by 196  parties back in 2015 but its climate  policies are  critically insufficient according to  climate action tracker  the country's emissions are the fourth  highest in the world  behind china the united states and india  but it continues to focus on development  and export of fossil fuels  mainly natural gas emissions in russia  are expected to carry on  at the same rate or to continue growing  until at least  2030 with the government's own targets  allowing them to increase  without missing international  commitments  yet the effects of climate change there  are stark  the arctic is warming twice as fast as  the rest of the world  and home to more than a fifth of the  world's forests  the country has seen two years of  record-breaking wildfires urban  populations are feeling the impact too  moscow recorded its hottest ever june  day  last month reaching just over 34 degrees  well with me now is our diplomatic  editor dominic  waghorn hello dominic and this is an  economy isn't it that's  deeply embedded in oil and gas revenues  it is but i think john kerry will be  going with a message that you need to  clean up your act for your own good  he'll probably point out that russia  is heating up faster than most of the  rest of the world two and a half times  uh the average of the rest of the world  that is leading to huge problems as you  say  forest fires huge flooding and also the  permafrost  flooding and that's doing things like  releasing methane but also anthrax  so there are real nightmare um scenarios  unfolding  in terms of environment in russia  already and the country is in the middle  of a heatwave so it's a good time for  john kerry to be going  there the good news is vladimir putin  seems to be noticing  this and worrying about it most recently  said in a speech that  mankind has got to minimize its impact  on the environment and the other good  news is that  uh joe biden and vladimir putin last  month kind of agreed to  agree on what they could agree on so to  set their relationship which has been at  a terrible low  on a more functional level it is huge  important for john kerry to make  progress there as you say russia  is has quite a small economy about the  size of italy's given the proportion of  the size of the country but it is the  fourth biggest carbon emitter  in the world so there's a lot of margin  in it climate margin if you like for  john kerry to  try and exploit there the bad news for  him is that vladimir putin has already  specifically said that foreign  governments  should not be using climate as a kind of  leverage to put pressure  on his own uh government his economy is  in a pretty polished state not least  because of western sanctions so it  doesn't kind of have  the sort of resources that might be  necessary to clean up its act  and the other bad news really for john  kerry is that actually climate change is  quite good for russia  in the north because it's melting the  arctic sea ice  and that is opening up that area to  exploitation for oil  and gas but also for shipping routes so  there's room for progress for john kerry  there  but i think he's going to have to manage  his expectations as far as to  what he's likely to get out of sergio  lavrov who he  he's meeting there delicate  conversations ahead dominic thank you  in today's other climate news severe  droughts hot weather and heavy rain  could all become more common in the uk  if global warming hits two degrees a  study published in the  journal climatic change found high  impact weather events will become more  frequent  and more intense as temperatures rise  severe droughts will become 86 more  likely  while snow days look less common as  temperatures below zero degrees  could decrease from 50 days down to 34.  greenhouse gas emissions from global  listed companies that could exceed  internationally agreed targets in just  six  years according to analysis by index  provider msci  it found many businesses would need to  drastically cut their emissions to keep  global warming  to 1.5 degrees above pre-industrial  levels  the analysis studied the climate  policies and disclosures of more than  9 000 listed companies  two blazes caused by lightning strikes  have combined to create the  largest wildfire of the year in  california  the fire which spans at least 134 square  miles  has been worsened by a heatwave the  second to hit  the northwest united states the extreme  heat has dried out surrounding  vegetation  making it flammable causing the fire to  spread rapidly  but an unlikely weapon being deployed by  firefighters comes in the form  of 80 goats by grazing on the tinder dry  grass in the region  these animals are helping to prevent the  fire from spreading further  protecting homes and wildlife there  well let's take a look at our data  dashboard now and the real-time  power mix in great britain essentially  where our electricity is coming from  well at the moment around 54  is coming from fossil fuels 26 from  renewable sources  and 20 coming from nuclear but if the  government  is to meet its target to reduce  greenhouse gas emissions by  78 by 2035 and to reach net zero by 2050  those numbers will have to change well  the national grid electricity system  operator has been looking at how energy  use in the uk  might change in the next three decades  they modeled four different scenarios  and found that the government's targets  can be met but only if consumers  embrace new ways to use power and urgent  action is taken  to increase energy efficiency measures  well joining me to take a look into the  future is matthew wright head of  strategy and regulation at national grid  eso  hello matthew just talk us through  what kind of things we all need to start  doing  over the next couple of years  uh good afternoon uh yes we we've  published our scenarios again this year  um as you say looking out to 2050.  um there are a number of key messages in  there but the one you highlight about  consumer involvement is arguably  arguably the most important  um our scenarios to hit net zero require  quite a number of lifestyle changes uh  we're all moving to electric vehicles  but we're going to need to have  smart charging of those vehicles uh  smart appliances in our house to make  use of our off-peak electricity  and there's even some changes required  to lifestyle habits  uh turning down thermostats and  obviously the stories that we hear about  changing our diets to some degree to to  reduce the amount of  red meat and other things that we eat  some of the bigger changes matthew  come at a cost don't they should the  government be  sharing in that cost  well i believe they already are so um if  we look at what's happened in recent  years  uh the growth of renewable power sources  for example offshore wind has been a it  has been a major  uh success story that's been driven by  the government providing  uh what's called contracts for  differences or subsidies to  to offshore wind in order to bring down  the costs of the that new form of  generation that's been spectacularly  successful i think going forward  we need similar sort of policy decisions  around  new technologies that would we will need  not least uh hydrogen  uh to replace uh natural gas for for  home heating  and indeed potentially for for making  electricity  uh and and potentially to fund energy  efficiency measures in people's homes to  bring  to bring up the thermal efficiency of  our housing stock  okay matthew wright good to talk to you  thank you you're welcome thank you  now in the latest in our series of  climate diaries  we're hearing from eliza droster the phd  student was part of a mission to take  the closest ever look at the arctic as  the epicenter of global warming  she told us what it was like to spend  months on a ship  frozen in ice  we had cake every single day  cake makes scientists happy and happy  scientists do good science  [Music]  the german icebreaker polar stern was  frozen  into the arctic sea ice and it drifted  along with the sea ice for an entire  year  scientists were rotated through every  few months  and i was on the final leg of this  expedition the arctic  ocean and especially the frozen arctic  ocean  is stunningly beautiful  there's this phenomenon where you have  some melt on the surface of the sea ice  and it creates these  beautiful melt ponds that have the most  fantastic colors of blue my research  um involved exploring the carbon dioxide  uptake of the arctic ocean  [Music]  how it changes throughout the seasons  and what it means  when we have less or no sea ice for me  this expedition was one  big epic scientific adventure  um it was really intense incredibly  exciting at the same time  it allowed me to personally experience  the vulnerability of the arctic  to a changing climate it's much more  impactful  to see this with your own eyes and  experiencing it with your own senses  instead of just seeing it in a graph  that's published  in a scientific article  an extraordinary experience and finally  cannabis  yes cannabis could be a possible  solution to climate change  hemp a specific variety of the plant is  being used to cut carbon emissions  in everything from fashion to  construction here's how  so  [Music]  well that is everything from us for  today next time  how fossil fuels could have a part to  play in a zero carbon future  find out how at the same time tomorrow  here on the daily climate show thanks  for watching

